  - [38;5;153mclient.py[39m: added [38;5;153mVERIFY[39m, read once from [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m (defaults to on), and passed it to both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m
    calls.
  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m: sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m alongside its base URL, so only that path skips the self-signed
    cert.
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m: untouched; it keeps full certificate verification.

  Checks: [38;5;153mruff format --check[39m clean on both changed files. [38;5;153mruff check[39m reports two [38;5;153mRUF100[39m unused-[38;5;153mnoqa[39m warnings on the
  [38;5;153mfrom client import ...[39m lines in both scripts — those are pre-existing from the initial commit and unrelated, so I left
  them.

  Two things worth knowing:

  - With [38;5;153mverify=False[39m, urllib3 prints an [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m per request. I didn't suppress it — the noise is
    arguably useful on a staging sync, but say the word if you want it silenced.
  - Since [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m is an env var and both scripts use [38;5;153msetdefault[39m, anything that exports [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m
    in the environment would disable verification for the prod export too. If that runner's environment isn't tightly
    controlled, a hardcoded flag passed into the client would be safer than an env var.

